# Lesson 4, batch 2: the lesson's Q95 opposite A82 layout in a trump contract. Draw trumps ending in your hand, lead toward dummy's queen; West has the king.
title: Toward the queen in trumps
id: L04-32
type: exercise
lesson: 4
concepts: 4.4
student: S
board: 118
dealer: E
vulnerable: EW

deal:            # spades  hearts  diamonds  clubs
  N  Q853   Q95    A54    763
  E  92     T763   T72    K854
  S  AKJ74  A82    K63    A2
  W  T6     KJ4    QJ98   QJT9

auction:
  E Pass
  S 1S     = 13+ points, at least four spades
     not *: 19 points and five spades: open your long suit at the one level.
  W Pass
  N 2S     = 6-9 points, 3+ spades
  E Pass
  S 4S     = Game
     not *: You have 20 points counting your doubleton, and partner has at least 6: enough for game.
  W Pass
  N Pass
  E Pass

auction recap:
  You opened 1♠ and partner raised to 2♠. With 20 points counting
  distribution, you bid game. You are declarer, because you bid spades
  first, and so West will lead to the first trick.

planning:
  trumps out: 4
  losers: 4
  spades: 0 = Nine spades with the ace, king and queen.
  hearts: 2 = Your ace takes care of one. Dummy's queen can take care of another, if West has the king and you lead toward it.
  diamonds: 1 = Your third diamond.
  clubs: 1 = Your small club.
  remind: You can afford three losers in 4♠. Count the obvious losers suit by suit in your own hand: spades, hearts, diamonds, clubs.
  remind: When can dummy's ♥Q win a trick? Which hand should lead hearts?
play:
  1   W CQ  N C3  E C4  S CA
      note: Four losers, one too many. Dummy's ♥Q can win a trick if West has the king and you lead hearts from your hand.
  2   S S4  W S6  N SQ  E S2
      note: Draw trumps. Dummy's queen first, the high card from the short side, then back to your hand.
  3   N S3  E S9  S SA  W ST
      note: Trumps are drawn, and you're in your hand, ready to lead a heart.
  4   S H2  W H4  N HQ*  E H3
      note: Lead a low heart toward dummy's queen.
      HQ key: West played low, so play the queen. If West has the king, the queen wins.
      HQ not *: Did you think about playing the queen? West has already played, so if West has the king, the queen wins now.
  5   N H5  E H6  S HA  W HJ
      note: The queen won. Cash your ace.
  6   S H8  W HK  N H9  E H7
      note: Your third heart is a loser.
  7   W CJ  N C6  E C5  S C2
      note: And your small club.
  8   W DQ  N DA  E D2  S D3
  9   N D4  E D7  S DK  W D8
  10  S D6  W DJ  N D5  E DT
      note: And your third diamond.
  11  W D9  N C7  E C8  S S7
      note: The rest are yours.
  12  S SK  W C9  N S5  E CK
  13  S SJ  W CT  N S8  E HT
      note: 10 tricks: 4♠ made.

play recap:
  The plan: four losers, one too many. Dummy's ♥Q could win a trick if West
  held the king and you led hearts from your hand.

  This is the same layout as in the lesson, in a trump contract. You drew
  trumps ending in your hand, then led toward the queen. West had to play
  first, and the queen won. Leading the queen from dummy would not have
  worked: whoever had the king would simply have covered it. If East had
  held the king, you would have lost two hearts and gone down one.
